        "text": "Estimate the mean of a normal population. The sample mean is 40 and n = 36. First suppose the population standard deviation σ = 6 is known.",
        "endMs": 5150
      },
      {
        "id": "watch-02",
        "startMs": 5150,
        "text": "On a Calculator page, choose Menu → Statistics → Confidence Intervals → z Interval. Select Stats rather than Data.",
        "endMs": 12200
      },
      {
        "id": "watch-03",
        "startMs": 12200,
        "text": "Enter σ = 6, x̄ = 40, n = 36, and C Level = 0.95. Enter a proportion, not 95.",
        "endMs": 22300
      },
      {
        "id": "watch-04",
        "startMs": 22300,
        "text": "Read CLower and CUpper: about 38.04 and 41.96. ME is the half-width, not an endpoint.",
        "endMs": 27000
      },
      {
        "id": "watch-05",
        "startMs": 27000,
        "text": "Now suppose 6 is only the sample SD, sx. Use t Interval with Stats. The distinction is whether σ is known, not whether n exceeds 30.",
        "endMs": 35000
      },
      {
        "id": "watch-06",
        "startMs": 35000,
        "text": "For t, enter x̄ first and sx second. Keep n = 36 and C Level = 0.95.",
        "endMs": 47100
      },
      {
        "id": "watch-07",
        "startMs": 47100,
        "text": "About 37.97 to 42.03 for the population mean. Over many repeated samples, this method would capture the true mean about 95% of the time.",
        "endMs": 52000
      }
    ],
